---
title: "Set Filter - Data Updates"
enterprise: true
---

This section describes how changing data through [Cell Editing](./cell-editing/) and the application [Updating Data](./data-update/) impacts the Set Filter's values. This is only applicable when the Set Filter is taking its values from the grid's data.

## Row / Cell Updates

Row / Cell updates refers to any of the following:

- All edits via the grid's UI such as [Cell Editing](./cell-editing/) and [Clipboard Operations](./clipboard/).
- Using the grid's [Single Row / Cell Update API](./data-update-single-row-cell/) i.e. `rowNode.setData(data)` and `rowNode.setDataValue(col,value)` API methods.

Filter Values will be refreshed when data is updated through any of these methods.

Here are the rules that determine how Filter Values are selected:

- **Filter Inactive**: Before the update 'all' values in the filter were selected (as the filter was inactive). The filter list will be updated reflecting the data change and all values will remain selected leaving the filter inactive
- **Filter Active**: Before the update 'some' values in the filter were selected (as the filter was active). The filter list will be updated reflecting the data change, however previous selections will remain intact as long as the filter value still exists after any transaction(s). If the update resulted in a new filter value, the new filter value will not be selected.

Cell editing does not re-execute filtering by default, so the row will not be filtered out even though the value in the cell is not selected in the filter. This default behaviour mimics how Excel works.

To execute filtering on cell edits, listen to `CellValueChanged` events and trigger filtering as shown below:

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-events/events.json" section="editing" names=["cellValueChanged"] /%}

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    onCellValueChanged: params => {
        // trigger filtering on cell edits
        params.api.onFilterChanged();
    }
}
```

The following example demonstrates Cell Editing with the Set Filter. Try the following:

**Without selecting any Filter Values:**

- Change (Cell Edit) a `'B'` cell value to `'X'` and note it gets added to the filter list and is **selected**.

**Click 'Reset' and deselect 'C' in the Filter List:**

- Change (Cell Edit) a `'B'` cell value to `'X'` and notice it gets added to the Filter List but it is **not selected**.
- Note that although `'X'` is not selected in the filter the row still appears in the grid. This is because grid filtering is not triggered for cell edits.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Cell Editing Updates" name="cell-editing-updates"  exampleHeight=480 /%}

## Transaction Updates

Transaction Updates refers to any of the following:

- Updating data via [Transactions](./data-update-transactions/) API.
- Updating data via [Async Transactions](./data-update-high-frequency/) API.
- Changes to Row Data and providing [Row ID](./row-ids/) (as this uses Transactions underneath the hood).

Filter values are refreshed when data is updated using any of these methods.

Here are the rules that determine how filter values are selected:

- **Filter Inactive**: Before the update 'all' values in the filter were selected (as the filter was inactive). The filter list will be updated reflecting the data change and all values will remain selected leaving the filter inactive.

- **Filter Active**: Before the update 'some' values in the filter were selected (as the filter was active). The filter list will be updated reflecting the data change, however previous selections will remain intact as long as the filter value still exists after any transaction(s). If the update resulted in a new filter value, the new filter value will not be selected.

Unlike [Cell Editing](#row--cell-updates), transaction updates will execute filtering in the grid.

The following example demonstrates these rules. Try the following:

**Without selecting any filter values:**

- Click **Update First Displayed Row**: this calls `api.applyTransaction()` and updates the value in the first row. Note `'AX'` now appears in the filter list and is **selected**.
- Click **Add New 'D' Row**: this calls `api.applyTransaction()` and adds a new row to the grid. Note `'D'` has been added to the filter list and is **selected**.

**Click 'Reset' and deselect 'C' in the Filter List:**

- Click **Update First Displayed Row**: this calls `api.applyTransaction()` and updates the value in the first row. Note `'AX'` now appears in the filter list and is **not selected**.
- Note that as `'AX'` is unselected in the filter list it has also been filtered out of the grid. This is because transaction updates also triggers grid filtering.
- Click **Add New 'D' Row**: this calls `api.applyTransaction()` and adds a new row to the grid. Note `'D'` has been added to the filter list and is **not selected**.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Transaction Updates" name="transaction-updates"  exampleHeight=480 /%}

## Setting New Data

When `api.setGridOption('rowData', data)` is called existing filter selections are kept when new rows are added. However it is possible to clear filter selections using: `api.setFilterModel(null)`.

The following example demonstrates how `api.setGridOption('rowData', data)` affects filter selections. Try the following:

- Deselect value 'A' from the set filter list and click the **Apply Data Update 1** button which calls `api.setGridOption('rowData', newData)` to add new data with extra rows to the grid.

- Notice 'A' remains deselected after new data is supplied to the grid.

- Clicking **Reset** invokes `api.setGridOption('rowData', origData)` to restore the original data but also clears any selections using `api.setFilterModel(null)`.

- Apply the filters so only 'B' is selected and click **Apply Data Update 1**. Notice how 'B' is no longer a filter value and therefore no longer in the filter list.
  Click **Apply Data Update 2** and notice how 'B' returns to the filter list but remains unselected.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Setting New Data" name="setting-new-data"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

## Preserving Previous Values

By default, a value that leaves the data is removed from the Filter List and its selected state is lost: if it later returns, it comes back as a new value. Set `preservePreviousValues` to keep values the filter has seen, together with whether they were selected. This suits filters that must survive data changing underneath them, for example:

- a saved or linked filter model applied before the rows it names have loaded;
- data that is cleared and reloaded, or replaced with `api.setGridOption('rowData', data)`;
- live updates and transactions that remove a value's rows and later add them back.

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="ISetFilterParams" names=["preservePreviousValues", "preservePreviousValuesLimit"] config={"description":""} /%}

With this enabled:

- A selected value whose rows are all removed stays selected, so its rows pass the filter again when they return.
- An unselected value whose rows are all removed stays unselected, so its rows stay filtered out when they return.
- Values no longer in the data are listed after the current values in a muted colour, and screen readers announce them as not in the current data. They can still be selected and deselected, and take part in the Mini Filter and **(Select All)**. In a [Tree List](./filter-set-tree-list/) they stay in place, and a group is muted once every value under it is no longer in the data.
- Values in a filter model that are not in the data are kept and listed by their key, so a model set before any rows are loaded is applied as written rather than reduced to the values present.
- The floating filter and the Filters Tool Panel include selected values that are no longer in the data, as the filter still selects them.
- New values are added as normal: unselected while the filter is active, and selected while it is inactive.
- Values supplied through `filterParams.values` or `setFilterValues` work the same way: a value missing from the latest list is kept.
- When values are taken from the grid, a value hidden only because another column's filter excludes its rows is still in the data, so it is not kept: it is hidden from the list as usual. A `values` callback that leaves out such values drops them from the provided values, so they are kept.

The following example demonstrates preserving previous values. Try the following:

- Click **Select Only B**, then **Apply Data Update 1**. `'B'` and `'C'` are no longer in the data but are still listed, muted. `'B'` stays selected, so no rows are shown, and the floating filter and the **Filter Summaries** panel still read `'B'`.
- Click **Apply Data Update 2**. `'B'` rows are shown again, and `'C'` rows stay filtered out as `'C'` is still unselected.
- Click **Apply Data Update 1** again, then **Clear Preserved Values** to discard `'B'` and `'C'`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Preserving Previous Values" name="preserve-previous-values"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

The [Advanced Filter](./filter-advanced/) only suggests current values, but still accepts a value that is out of the data. In the following example, click **Apply Data Update 1**, then type `[Set Filter Column] is any of ["B"]` and apply it: `'B'` is no longer in the data and is not suggested, but it is accepted and no rows are shown. Click **Apply Data Update 2** and the `'B'` rows are shown again. The Advanced Filter has no filter model per column, so `preservePreviousValuesLimit` applies to every value it keeps, including values used in the applied expression.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Preserving Previous Values with the Advanced Filter" name="preserve-previous-values-advanced-filter"  exampleHeight=400 /%}

In a [Tree List](./filter-set-tree-list/) on a group column, a value no longer in the data stays in its group, muted, and a group is muted once every value under it is gone. A model value that was never in the data is listed at the root by its key. The following examples show this with [Row Grouping](./grouping/) and [Tree Data](./tree-data/). Open the filter from the floating filter's button, then try the following:

- Click **Select Paris and Madrid** (or **Select report.pdf and old.zip**). `'Madrid'` (or `'old.zip'`) is not in the data, so it is listed at the root by its key.
- Click **Remove Rome** (or **Remove beach.jpg**). The value stays in its group, muted and unselected.
- Click **Remove France** (or **Remove Documents**). The whole group is muted, and `'Paris'` (or `'report.pdf'`) stays selected.
- Click **Restore Data**, then **Clear Preserved Values** after removing values to discard them.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Preserving Previous Values with Row Grouping" name="preserve-previous-values-grouping"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

{% gridExampleRunner title="Preserving Previous Values with Tree Data" name="preserve-previous-values-tree-data"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

### Limiting Preserved Values

Selected values are always kept. Unselected values that are no longer in the data are kept up to `preservePreviousValuesLimit`:

| `preservePreviousValuesLimit` | Unselected values kept once they leave the data                                                  |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| not set                       | up to 100                                                                                        |
| a number, for example `500`   | up to that number                                                                                |
| `0`                           | none: only selected values are kept, so the list shows the current values plus the selected ones |
| `-1`                          | all of them, with no limit                                                                       |

When the limit is exceeded, the value that left the data first is discarded first. Discarding a value never changes which rows pass the filter: an unselected value is not in the model, so if its rows return it is added back as a new, unselected value and its rows stay filtered out, exactly as if it had been kept. The limit only decides which of these values stay listed, and in memory, while their rows are away.

Values are discarded when the values next reload, for example when the data changes or `refreshFilterValues` is called, not when a value is deselected. Only the applied filter model protects a value: with an **Apply** button, a value selected but not yet applied can be discarded by such a reload.

While the filter is inactive there is no model, so every retained value counts as unselected and the limit still applies. If a filter is applied later, a discarded value that returns is added back as a new, unselected value.

### Clearing Preserved Values

Call `clearPreservedValues()` on the filter handler to discard every preserved value that is not in the current data:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
api.getColumnFilterHandler('col1').clearPreservedValues();
```

This reconciles the filter model with the remaining values, as happens without `preservePreviousValues`: selected values that were discarded leave the model, and the filter becomes inactive if every remaining value is selected.

Pass `true` to discard only the values that are not in the applied filter model. Those in it stay listed, and the model is left as it is:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
api.getColumnFilterHandler('col1').clearPreservedValues(true);
```

This is the column filter's handler, so it does not reach the values the [Advanced Filter](./filter-advanced/) keeps.

### Lifetime and Memory

A column with `preservePreviousValues` creates its filter when the grid starts, rather than when the filter is first used, so it sees values before they leave. This also applies to a Set Filter inside a [Multi Filter](./filter-multi/). A selectable filter only creates the filter that is selected, so a Set Filter there keeps values only while it is selected. A `values` callback is therefore called when the grid starts. Values are kept for the life of the filter: they are discarded if the filter is destroyed, for example with `api.destroyFilter`, after which the filter starts again from the current data. They are also discarded when the rules that make their keys change: `caseSensitive`, or for a [Tree List](./filter-set-tree-list/) on a group column that takes its values from the grid, the row group columns, `groupAllowUnbalanced` or Tree Data. The last three change the keys themselves, so model values that no longer match any value leave the model. They are not saved with [Grid State](./grid-state/): a saved model restores the selected values, and unselected values stay filtered out because they are not in the model.

Keeping values also keeps them in memory. Columns whose values change constantly, such as timestamps, unique IDs or frequently edited cells, add a value for every change, and a value that is a complex object keeps that object alive. Lower `preservePreviousValuesLimit` for such columns, or set it to `0` to keep only what is selected.
